  "teaching": {
    "task": "# Year 12 Physics M5 V22: Orbital energy. Why does a higher circular orbit have lower speed but greater total energy? (design)",
    "causalExplanation": "Let's build the satellite's energy one piece at a time. First, kinetic energy. In a circular orbit, gravity supplies the centripetal force, so v squared equals big G M over r. Put that into a half m v squared, and the kinetic energy is big G M m over two r. It's positive, and it gets smaller as r gets bigger, because higher orbits are slower. Notice the satellite's mass doesn't cancel this time, so energy does depend on m. Next, potential energy. From last time, U is minus big G M m over r, with zero at infinity. Compare the sizes: U is exactly twice as big as the kinetic energy, and it's negative. Higher up, U is less negative, so moving out, U rises while K falls. Now add them. Big G M m over two r, plus minus big G M m over r, gives a total energy of minus big G M m over two r. It's negative, which means the satellite is bound: it doesn't have enough energy to reach infinity. And here's the key. A bigger r makes this total less negative, so a higher orbit has more total energy. In fact, the total is exactly minus the kinetic energy. Lose speed, and the total goes up.",
    "conversationalApproach": "A five hundred kilogram satellite is in a circular orbit, seven thousand kilometres from Earth's centre, moving at about seven point five six kilometres per second. To lift it higher, its engine pushes it forwards, and it speeds up. But once it settles into a circular orbit twice as far out, it's moving at only five point three five kilometres per second, slower than when it started. The engine added energy. So where did it go? Have a think. Let's build the satellite's energy one piece at a time. First, kinetic energy. In a circular orbit, gravity supplies the centripetal force, so v squared equals big G M over r. Put that into a half m v squared, and the kinetic energy is big G M m over two r. It's positive, and it gets smaller as r gets bigger, because higher orbits are slower. Notice the satellite's mass doesn't cancel this time, so energy does depend on m. Next, potential energy. From last time, U is minus big G M m over r, with zero at infinity. Compare the sizes: U is exactly twice as big as the kinetic energy, and it's negative. Higher up, U is less negative, so moving out, U rises while K falls.",
    "openingDecision": "opens on v21's final frame (see \"Opening frame\"); the bars fade and the inner satellite plate moves on its circle (r₁ = 7.0 × 10⁶ m) with a teal speed readout (52 px) \"7.56 km s⁻¹\" and a live teal speed bar. On \"speeds up\" a short green thrust arrow fires along the velocity for a moment and the speed bar jumps up, tag \"faster\"; the satellite leaves the circle on a dashed outward path, and as it climbs the bar falls, tag \"slowing as it climbs\"; reaching r₂ it is too slow for the new circle (tag \"slower than this circle needs\"), and a second short green arrow fires. On \"second\" it runs on the outer circle (r₂ = 1.4 × 10⁷ m), readout \"5.35 km s⁻¹\"; a red chip \"slower than at the start\". Hand tags (30 px): green \"engine adds energy\", teal \"7.56 → 5.35\", and a teal pen ring round \"5.35 km s⁻¹\". On \"think\" the 3 s `Countdown` runs beside \"The engine added energy. Where did it go?\" with three option chips (36 px) \"into speed\", \"into height\", \"it was lost\".",
    "understandingCheck": "Your turn. An eight hundred kilogram satellite is in a circular orbit five hundred kilometres above Earth's surface. Calculate its kinetic energy, its gravitational potential energy and its total energy, using the data sheet values. Pause here to answer. Last one. A satellite in a low circular orbit slowly loses energy to air drag, and gradually spirals down through lower circular orbits. Explain why its speed increases, even though drag removes energy. Pause here to answer.",
    "curriculumScope": "# Year 12 Physics M5 V22: Orbital energy. Why does a higher circular orbit have lower speed but greater total energy? (design)\n\nStatus: Revision 3 (13 Nov 2026): visual-standard top-up (stage list, question-card pictures, givens strips, five-line notes, simulation engines named, root rules R1 to R4), visuals only. Narration unchanged. Needs a Codex error check before plates and voicing. Previously Revision 2 (6 Nov 2026): brief 188 findings resolved and visual upgrade to maths-visual-standard.md.\n\nNarration 5,554 characters (scene sum; Revision 1 stated 5,569 by the joined convention), 16 scenes, about 504 s, counted by script with the build parser rules (see \"Narration length estimate\").\n\nScope: Year 12 Physics (Physics Stage 6, 2017 syllabus), Module 5 (Advanced Mechanics), inquiry question \"How does the force of gravity determine the motion of planets and satellites?\". Templates as `phys-y12-m5-v17.md`. No pre-design pack exists; the pack work is done here. Site lesson read in full (6 Nov, read-only): `C:/Users/kygs/teaching-app-fix/subjects/physics/year12/module5/lesson12.sa.html`, SHA256 `441719a2abd13157d21f73bb1213e999f7ee9bde8f45feada17655abc31ca73e` (stale: hashed against the old C:/Users/kygs/hscscience copy; not re-hashed against teaching-app-fix, 24 Nov 2026) (recomputed 6 Nov; matches the route). Route entry: `phys-y12-m5-v22`. Route start: \"Start from the physical case in the learner question; recall only the earlier result needed for it.\" Route stop: \"Combine kinetic and potential energy into E=-GMm/(2r); separate the instantaneous transfer burn from the final circular state. Stop before detailed transfer ellipses.\" Next: \"phys-y12-m5-v23: What minimum launch speed allows escape without further propulsion?\"\n\nOwner rules applied: as v17 (2017 syllabus quoted exactly; no people, hands, faces or body outlines; Earth, the satellite and the comet are painted plates; energy bars, curves, thrust arrows and strips are